  title: "Compiler and Kernel Software — How Code Reaches the Hardware",
  date: "2026-09-16",
  body: `Prior Lessons — 1-Sentence Recap

Lesson 036: More accelerators create value only when memory, packaging, deployment and software can also turn them into useful AI work.

Lesson 037: A GPU performs many matrix calculations in parallel, but idle math units do not produce useful work.

Lesson 038: Caches and local storage keep reused data near the math units, reducing slower trips to HBM.

Lesson 039: Using fewer bits can make AI faster and cheaper, provided the smaller numbers preserve the quality the application needs.

Lesson 040: GPUs favor flexibility while custom chips favor efficiency, so the better design depends on the workload and its software.

The big idea

A model framework describes what math should happen. It does not directly tell billions of transistors when to multiply, move data or wait.

Compilers, libraries, kernels and runtimes translate that model into hardware work. Better software can combine operations, reuse memory and keep compute units busy; poor software can make an excellent accelerator look slow.

Where we are

model framework → captured operation graph → compiler → library or generated kernel → runtime and driver → accelerator

1. The framework describes the job

In a framework such as PyTorch, a developer writes operations on tensors: multiply matrices, add a bias, apply an activation or normalize values.

In eager execution, the framework can run operations as Python reaches them. That helps experimentation, but seeing one operation at a time limits optimization across the model.

A compiler tries to capture a region as a graph: operations are nodes and tensors connect them. PyTorch's torch.compile finds compilable regions; a graph break ends one region and resumes later. Larger graphs provide more optimization opportunities, though changing inputs may require recompilation.

2. The compiler turns intent into a plan

The captured graph is lowered into an intermediate representation: a hardware-independent description that is easier to analyze than Python code.

XLA uses High Level Optimizer, or HLO, operations. Compiler passes can remove redundant work, choose data layouts, trade recomputation for lower memory use and fuse compatible operations.

The compiler then selects a tuned library implementation or generates code for a specific accelerator. Its plan must respect supported data types, registers, shared memory and thread organization.

This is why the same model and chip can produce different performance after a software update. The hardware did not change; the translation did.

3. A kernel is the unit sent to the accelerator

Here, kernel does not mean an operating-system kernel. It is a small accelerator program that performs an operation, or a fused group of operations, across many data elements.

A matrix kernel divides matrices into tiles, assigns thread blocks, stages data in fast memory and accumulates results. Those choices affect reuse, traffic and occupancy.

Developers often use libraries instead of writing every kernel. NVIDIA's cuDNN accepts operation graphs and selects execution engines, including fused kernels for supported patterns.

The runtime launches kernels with parameters such as grid size, thread-block size and stream. The driver submits that work to the device, whose scheduler assigns thread blocks to available compute units.

4. Why fusion can matter

Consider three simple operations applied in sequence to a 100-megabyte tensor. If each separate kernel reads 100 megabytes from HBM and writes 100 megabytes back, the three kernels move about 600 megabytes.

